# Visible-Light-Responsive 2D Cadmium-Organic Framework Single Crystals with Dual Functions of Water Reduction and Oxidation

## abstract
The development of new metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with dual functions of both water reduction and oxidation under visible-light irradiation is highly desirable for promising solar water splitting, but is not yet reported. Herein, a cadmium-based MOF (denoted as Cd-TBAPy) single crystal with a 2D layered framework by employing 1,3,6,8-tetrakis(p-benzoic acid)pyrene (H4 TBAPy) as an organic linker is reported, which exhibits good visible-light absorption with edge of 600 nm. The Mott-Schottky (M-S) measurement and UV-vis analysis integrally reveal that the Cd-TBAPy is an n-type semiconductor with a bandgap of 2.15 eV whose conduction and valence band are estimated to be -0.05 and 2.10 eV, respectively. Together with loading of Pt or CoPi cocatalyst, the Cd-TBAPy is active for both water reduction and oxidation in the presence of scavengers under visible-light irradiation. Especially, the optimized apparent quantum efficiency for O2 evolution reaches 5.6% at 420 nm, much higher than that of previous MOF-based photocatalysts reported so far. This is thought to be the first MOF that functions as a photocatalyst for both water reduction and oxidation under visible light, demonstrating the intriguing future of MOF materials in solar-to-chemical energy conversion.

The development of new metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with dual functions of both water reduction and oxidation under visiblelight irradiation is highly desirable for promising solar water splitting, but is not yet reported. Herein, a cadmium-based MOF (denoted as “Cd-TBAPy”) single crystal with a 2D layered framework by employing 1,3,6,8-tetrakis(p-benzoic acid)pyrene $\{H_{4}\mathsf{T B A P y}\}$ as an organic linker is reported, which exhibits good visible-light absorption with edge of $\approx600\ \mathsf{n m}$ . The Mott–Schottky (M–S) measurement and UV–vis analysis integrally reveal that the Cd-TBAPy is an n-type semiconductor with a bandgap of ${\approx}2.15$ eV whose conduction and valence band are estimated to be $-0.05$ and $2.10\mathrm{eV},$ respectively. Together with loading of Pt or CoPi cocatalyst, the Cd-TBAPy is active for both water reduction and oxidation in the presence of scavengers under visible-light irradiation. Especially, the optimized apparent quantum efficiency for $\b_{0_{2}}$ evolution reaches $5.6\%$ at $420\ \mathsf{n m}$ , much higher than that of previous MOF-based photocatalysts reported so far. This is thought to be the first MOF that functions as a photocatalyst for both water reduction and oxidation under visible light, demonstrating the intriguing future of MOF materials in solar-to-chemical energy conversion.
Metal–organic frameworks composed of metal ions or clusters and polydentate organic linkers have been widely investigated for gas storage and separation, sensor, drug delivery, and catalysis.[1] The MOFs materials have been known to possess many attractive properties, such as large surface areas, easy structural and functional adjustment, and porosity.[2] In recent years, rational design and functional modification have also demonstrated potential of MOFs in photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, organic synthesis, or water splitting.[3]
Solar-driven water splitting is of great significance in consideration that the hydrogen evolution provides an environmental friendly and sustainable alternative to replace fossil fuels,[4] toward which the exploration of new visiblelight-responsive photocatalysts with suitable band edge positions is always important and desirable. Although over hundreds of inorganic semiconductors have been developed as photocatalysts for solar water splitting during the past decades,[5] continuous efforts on developing visible-light-driven photocatalysts are still requisite. Compared to the limitation of numbered elements in designing and synthesizing inorganic semiconductor photocatalysts, the MOFs-related materials   
exhibit much more possibilities in developing novel materials   
for promising solar water splitting due to the diverse choice of ic linkers and metal nodes as well as their flexible coordi  
nation modes.[3a,b,d] For example, UiO-66 and Al-TA have been   
early reported as typical photocatalysts for water reduction and   
oxidation under the excitation of UV light respectively.[6] In order   
to extend the utilization of visible light occupying over $40\%$ solar   
spectrum, some visible-light-responsive MOFs have been thus   
explored as photocatalysts, such as $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ –Ti–MOF and Al–PMOF   
for water reduction,[7] and Bi-mna for water oxidation.[8] Howthese as-developed MOFs can only drive water reduction ter oxidation under visible light. To our knowledge, MOFs dual functions of both water reduction and oxidation under   
visible-light irradiation has not been reported yet, although great s have been made to exploit potential MOFs photocatalysts.
The dual functions of water reduction and oxidation are essential for one-step overall water splitting that can effectively convert solar energy into chemical one.[4,5] To reach this goal, the promising MOFs candidate is first required to own suitable conduction and valence bands that should straddle the potentials of water reduction and oxidation to satisfy their thermodynamic requirements respectively. Second, it is desirable to have a narrow bandgap for good visible-light harvesting. Additionally, it is also expected to be of robustness with good resistance to photocorrosion. It is general to know that the above properties are greatly dependent on the structure of MOFs shaped by the coordination of metal ions and organic linkers,[7a,8,9] so the choice of these two components is crucial for the modification of its band edge positions, visible-light-responsive property and stability.
Herein, using 1,3,6,8-tetrakis(p-benzoic acid)pyrene $\mathrm{(H_{4}T B A P y)}$ as an organic linker, we report a novel stable cadmium–organic framework (Cd-TBAPy) with absorption edge of ${\approx}600\ \mathrm{nm}$ to exhibit dual functions of water reduction and oxidation under visible light. The $\mathrm{H_{4}T B A P y}$ was employed here as linker in consideration that it belongs to the typical pyrene-based organic derivatives that have been known to not only possess large electron delocalization systems for favorable intense visible-light absorption, but also own feasible energy transfer ability.[10,11] Based on Mott–Schottky and UV–vis measurements, the conduction and valence band edge positions of Cd-TBAPy are evaluated to well satisfy the thermodynamic requirement of water reduction and oxidation respectively. After loading of suitable cocatalysts such as $\mathrm{Pt}$ and CoPi, the Cd-TBAPy MOFs photocatalyst exhibit effective hydrogen and oxygen evolution under visible-light irradiation in the presence of TEOA and ${\tt A g N O}_{3}$ , respectively. It is worth noting that the apparent quantum efficiency (AQE) of water oxidation reaches $5.6\%$ at $420\ \mathrm{nm}$ , to the best of our knowledge, which is the highest AQE value among the MOFs-related photocatalysts reported to date.
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction analyses reveal that the Cd-TBAPy is a 2D layered framework crystallized in triclinic space group P-1. The microscope image of Cd-TBAPy crystals with cubic structure and side length of $300{\mathrm{-}}400~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ is shown in Figure S1 of the Supporting Information and details of the crystal structure and refinement data are provided in Table S1 of the Supporting Information. As shown in Figure 1a, the Cd atom is coordinated with eight oxygen atoms from four different linkers, and the bond length of Cd-O is ${\approx}2.348\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . The coordination between Cd atoms and $\mathrm{H_{4}T B A P}$ y linkers shapes the backbone of Cd-TBAPy into 2D sheets (Figure 1b), and all single layers are orderly stacked to form a 2D eclipsed structure with a interlayer spacing of ${\approx}4.28$ Å (Figure 1c). 1D channels of about $7.69\times10.81\mathrm{~\AA}^{2}$ along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -axis exist, and the total solvent-accessible volume is ${\approx}18.2\%$ upon solvent removal, which is calculated with PLATON software.[12] Meanwhile, the BET surface area and the Langmuir surface area of Cd-TBAPy is measured to be 27 and $38~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. It is still unclear why the surface area of the 2D MOFs is relatively low, but similar observation is found for the MOFs with structure of channels in previous works.[8,13] The experimental XRD pattern (Figure 1d) of Cd-TBAPy matches very well with the simulated one which is generated on the basis of single crystal structure analysis, showing a good phase purity of the bulk material.
The coordination effect between cadmium ions and $\mathrm{H_{4}T B A P y}$ linkers can be well confirmed by comparing the FT-IR (Figure 2a) and SSNMR $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 2b) spectrum of the Cd-TBAPy and $\mathrm{H_{4}T B A P y}$ samples. Compared to the $\mathrm{H_{4}T B A P y}$ sample, the hydroxyl vibration peak of $-\mathrm{COOH}$ in the range of $3000{-}3400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ disappears for the Cd-TBAPy together with shift of the peak ascribed to the vibration of carbonyl group in $-\mathrm{COOH}$ in the range of $1620{-}1750\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to lower wavenumber. The SSNMR $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ peak of carbon atom in $-\mathrm{COOH}$ of $\mathrm{H_{4}T B A P y}$ is also apparently shifted after the addition of cadmium ions. The split of the peak located at 176.8 and 174.6 indicates the formation of two different coordination environments in $-\mathrm{COOH}$ groups as the coordination with cadmium ions. The framework collapse of as-coordinated Cd-TBAPy sample occurs until over $653~\mathrm{K}$ according to thermogravimetric analysis curve (Figure 2c), and the no obvious change of XRD patterns can be observed for samples before and after impregnation in acetonitrile, methylene dichloride, water, DMF or acetone for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature (Figure 2d). All of them indicate that the as-obtained Cd-TBAPy MOFs owns excellent thermal and solvent stability.
The UV/vis DRS of Cd-TBAPy shows a broad range of absorption in the visible region from 400 to $600\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 3a), based on which its bandgap is calculated to be ${\approx}2.15$ eV, much smaller than some reported MOFs, such as MIL-125 $(3.6\ \mathrm{eV})$ , UiO-66 (3.05 eV), UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ $(2.8\mathrm{eV})$ , and MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (2.6 eV).[6a,14] In order to further evaluate the band edge positions of CdTBAPy, Mott–Schottky plot of the Cd-TBAPy electrode was also measured in phosphate buffer solution with $\mathrm{\pH}$ value of ${\approx}7.0$ , close to the environment for photocatalytic reaction tests. The result shown in Figure  3b reveals the n-type semiconductor property of Cd-TBAPy, and the flat band potential is calculated to be $_{\approx0.15\mathrm{~V~}}$ versus RHE. It is generally known that the bottom of the conduction band of a n-type semiconductor is more negative by ${\approx}0.20~\mathrm{eV}$ than the flat band potential,[15] so the conduction band minimum of Cd-TBAPy is estimated to be $\approx-0.05\ \mathrm{eV}.$ Considering the band gap value of $2.15\ \mathrm{eV}$ has been estimated from the UV/vis DRS (Figure 3a), the valence band maximum should be located at $2.10\ \mathrm{eV}$ which is in good accordance with the value detected by XPS (Figure S2, Supporting Information). The band edge positions of Cd-TBAPy are estimated and shown in the inset of Figure  3b, in which both the conduction and valence band possess sufficient potentials for the water reduction and oxidation, respectively.
Encouraged by its excellent thermal and solvent stability (particular in water), wide visible-light absorption, and suitable band positions for water splitting, the Cd-TBAPy material is highly expected for potential solar water splitting. Accordingly, the photocatalytic performances of Cd-TBAPy for water reduction and oxidation under visible-light irradiation were examined by using TEOA and ${\tt A g N O}_{3}$ as hole and electron scavengers, respectively. As for water reduction, no obvious $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution on the Cd-TBAPy itself is detected in dark. As given in Figure S3a of the Supporting Information, the loading of suitable cocatalyst is favorable for the water reduction, among which the loaded platinum exhibits the most efficient promotion on the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate. A volcano-type curve of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution dependence on the Pt content is observed (Figure S3b, Supporting Information), and the optimal $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of
$4.3~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ appears for the sample with loading of $3.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pt. Figure 3c shows the typical time course of photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution on the optimized sample under visible-light irradiation, in which the linear increase of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution as a function of reaction time demonstrates its good photochemical stability. Based on the binding energies of Pt 4f XPS spectrum of CdTBAPy after hydrogen evolution, it is known that the metallic Pt has been mainly loaded on the surface of Cd-TBAPy to act as cocatalyst (Figure S4a, Supporting Information).[16]
Similarly, loading of CoPi is demonstrated to well promote its $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ evolution rate, and a volcano-type curve of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ evolution as a function of the amount of CoPi loaded is also observed (Figure  S3c, Supporting Information). Similar peaks of $\mathrm{Co}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Co}2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ XPS spectrum as previous CoPi loaded can be seen for the CoPi-modified Cd-TBAPy sample (Figure  S4b, Supporting Information).[17] The sample with loading of $0.4\mathrm{wt\%}$ CoPi exhibits the optimal $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ evolution rate of $81.7~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , and the AQE of water oxidation measured under $420\pm10~\mathrm{nm}$ irradiation is $5.6\%$ . In addition, the high dependence of photocatalytic $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ evolution rate on the wavelength of the irradiation light indicates that the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ evolution is driven by the visible light (Figure  3d). It is worth noting that the reaction of water oxidation on the $0.4\mathrm{\wt{\%}\ C o P i/\Omega}$ Cd-TBAPy still occurs under the irradiation with the cutoff wavelength of $580\ \mathrm{nm}$ $(2580\ \mathrm{nm})$ , demonstrating its wide visible-light utilization. As a comparison, the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ evolution rates of water oxidation on previous typical visible-light-responsive MOFs photocatalysts are given in Table S2 of the Supporting Information, among which our photocatalyst shows the best activity. It is worth noting that the structures of photocatalysts before and after reactions are not obviously changed (Figure  S5, Supporting Information), demonstrating its good photochemical stability. Additionally, the transient photocurrent response on the Cd-TBAPy photoelectrode in a three-electrode set-up was carried out by using phosphate buffer solution as electrolyte solution. An obvious photoanode current can be observed from the chopped photocurrent profile of Cd-TBAPy (Figure S6, Supporting Information), indicating the generation of electron–hole pairs in Cd-TBAPy upon light irradiation, and the capacity for photoelectrochemical water oxidation by photo­ induced holes. The positive photocurrent also suggests that the Cd-TBAPy material is an n-type semiconductor.
In order to get insights into its intriguing photocatalytic water splitting performances, the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) measurements were conducted to evaluate the effect of cocatalysts. As shown in Figure 4a the steady-state PL intensity of Cd-TBAPy with the loading of Pt or CoPi cocatalyst is remarkably decreased compared to the pristine sample, demonstrating efficient electron transfer and separation between CdTBAPy and cocatalyst Pt or CoPi. The promoted carrier separation should be an important parameter to cause enhanced water reduction and oxidation activities. Second, the loading of cocatalysts has been widely known to accelerate the activation and conversion of water molecule,[18] so the loading of suitable cocatalysts should be another crucial factor to ensure the outstanding photocatalytic performance of Cd-TBAPy. It should be pointed out that the Pt or CoPi loaded on the surface of Cd-TBAPy is not active for water reduction or water oxidation under light irradiation, but it can act as cocatalyst to accelerate the charge separation of the MOFs photocatalyst and the surface catalysis reaction kinetics. Additionally, as given in
Figure S7 of the Supporting Information, the loaded platinum nanocrystals with main size of $2{\-}4~\mathrm{nm}$ are homogeneously dispersed, while the loaded CoPi mainly exist as amorphous nanoparticles on the surface of Cd-TBAPy. Third, it has been known that the pyrene part in $\mathrm{H_{4}T B A P y}$ is a polycyclic aromatic conjugated polymer with a planer structure which contains abundant $\pi$ electrons.[10,11] In this work, the pyrene parts in each plane of the 2D layer parallel with each other leading to the overlap of $\pi$ -orbitals, then the eclipsed stacking of 2D layers is expected to induce a strong electronic coupling between the overlapped $\pi$ -orbitals to form $\pi$ -conjugated system which can facilitate the transportation of photoinduced carriers,[12,19] as should be another factor resulting in the excellent photocatalytic performance of Cd-TBAPy.
The presence of $\pi$ -conjugated system in Cd-TBAPy can be identified by the calculated result of its molecular orbital composition. As shown in Figure  4b, the bottom of conduction band and the top of valence band are mainly composed of C 2p orbitals and O 2p orbitals, respectively. The conduction band is composed entirely of $\mathrm{~C~}2\mathrm{p}$ orbitals which can be further attributed to C p- $\pi$ antibonding orbitals according to the binding energy position,[20] thus confirms the existence of $\pi$ -conjugated system in Cd-TBAPy. Furthermore, the contribution of $\mathrm{Cd~4d}$ and 5s orbitals is negligible compared with O $\mathsf{2p}$ and $\mathrm{~C~}2\mathrm{p}$ orbitals originating from organic linkers, due to its full 4d orbitals and empty 5s orbitals. That means the critical role of organic linker in band composition and positions, so the rational selection of $\mathrm{H_{4}T B A P y}$ is vital for Cd-TBAPy to achieve both water reduction and oxidation. Based on above discussion, the possible mechanism of Cd-TBAPy for water reduction and oxidation is illustrated and shown in Figure  5. First, the Cd-MOFs absorb visible light to generate electrons and holes, which will be then transferred into the cocatalysts Pt or CoPi due to their different energy alignment. Afterward, the deposited Pt and CoPi will act as active sites to carry out the catalytic reduction and oxidation of water, respectively.
In summary, we synthesize a new cadmium–organic framework (Cd-TBAPy) with 2D layered framework and visible-light absorption edge of ${\approx}600$ nm for both water reduction and oxidation under visible-light irradiation. After loading suitable cocatalysts, the hydrogen and oxygen evolution rate can reach up to 4.3 and $81.7~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. The optimal AQE of water oxidation under $420\ \mathrm{nm}$ irradiation reaches $5.6\%$ . Its inherent band edge positions, the loading of suitable cocatalysts and the $\pi$ -conjugated 2D layered structure of Cd-TBAPy are expected to integrally contribute to the extraordinary photocatalytic performance of Cd-TBAPy. To our knowledge, this is the first MOF material which can fulfill both visible-light-driven water reduction and oxidation. Our results not only add a new member to the family of MOF material, but also highlight the promising future of MOFs in solar water splitting. From the view point of solar-to-chemical energy conversion, the fabrication of overall water splitting system on the basis of it is highly expected, considering its satisfying thermodynamic band structures and effective bifunctional half-reaction kinetics. Further efforts may be laid on the controllable synthesis, effective surface/interface modification and deep understanding to correlation between structures and performance, all of which are under investigation.
